          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'The basic game is for two players, using three suits from a standard 52-card pack.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the most points by capturing the highest-value prize cards (Diamonds).',
          gameplay: '1. Reveal: The top card of the Diamond (Prize) pile is turned face-up.\n2. Bid: Both players choose one card from their hand and place it face-down.\n3. Showdown: Both bid cards are revealed simultaneously.\n4. Award: The player with the higher bidding card wins the Diamond card and adds its value to their score.\n5. Discard: Both bidding cards are discarded regardless of who won.',
          keyRules: [
            'Symmetry: Because both players have the same cards, the only difference is the order in which they play them.',
            'No Re-use: Once you play your King, it is gone. If you waste your King on a 1ndpoint Ace, you have lost a massive strategic asset.',
            'Winning condition: Win the most points by capturing the highest-value prize cards (Diamonds).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Conserve your high cards for high prizes. Bidding a Jack for a 2 of Diamonds is almost always a mistake, as you leave yourself vulnerable when the King of Diamonds eventually appears.',
          intermediate: "The 'N+1' Rule: Try to beat your opponent's likely bid by exactly one point. If you think they will play a 7 for an 8-prize, play an 8. Winning a trick by 1 point is the hallmark of efficient GOPS play.",
          advanced: "Tie Manipulation: If a low-value prize is up (like a 3), and you suspect a tie will happen, you might deliberately play a very low card (like an Ace) to 'save' a midndtier card, knowing that you can fight for the rolledndover prize in the next round.",
          tips: [
            'Pay attention to what cards your opponent has already used. If they have already played their King and Queen, and you still have yours, you are in a dominant position for the final rounds.',
            "If you cannot win a prize, play your lowest possible card (the 'trash card' strategy) to minimize your loss of bidding power.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
